WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Tide's in the room, turn something.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Tide, the watch-day miracle that gives you the cleanest clothes in town,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:16.000
brings you in person, direct from Hollywood, that lady-lovable clown of clowns, Red Skelton!

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:35.000
T-I-D-E, Tide's in the room, Red Skelton!

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Thank you very much, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
I brought these out in case some of you folks at home haven't seen the Emmy.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.000
And I would like to thank the ladies and gentlemen of the press,

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.000
and also the ladies and gentlemen of the Academy for voting our show twice.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.000
One is they liked our show, and the other is they like me as a comedian.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Well, you've seen them now, so, Ed, do you want to thank them?

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
You know, it was really quite a thrill to get something like that, you know.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.000
And over at the Academy, it's a funny thing, as I walked in,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
there were a lot of people outside, and someone yelled,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Red Skelton's in the crowd!

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.000
And they all turned around and looked at me.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Gee, I was so embarrassed.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.000
I was sorry I yelled.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
The autograph collectors, they had a cute idea.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
For the stars, they would let them sign full sheets of paper,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.000
and for the pit players, a half a sheet of paper.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Did you ever try to autograph a spitball?

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.000
And a little Richard this week, he came in, they want pet mice.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.000
I said, what do you want with mice?

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
They said they don't brush their teeth, they don't take a bath or anything.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.000
He said, don't tell one of them to move over, he's got a new buddy.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.000
They went over to Gene Fowler's house the other day,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
and they raided his cuckoo clock.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.000
This is the truth.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.000
He took a little cuckoo out of the clock, so I said, why did you do that?

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.000
He said, well, I didn't mean no harm by it, you know.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.000
He said, I saw the cuckoo in there, and I thought maybe I could get some cuckoo eggs

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.000
and raise my own clock.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.000
There's an interesting bulletin I just saw in the paper today

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.000
that MacArthur, they've called all of his men but three.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:27.000
They figure if he won't play ball, he can't play canasta either.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.000
This week while I was watching television, I happened to notice one of the fights that was on,

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.000
and I didn't pay so much attention to the fight as I did to the people who were sitting around the ringside.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.000
So I'd like to do a little pantomime scene for you

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.000
and show you how different people react to fights by, could I have that?

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Thing's got legs, why didn't you let it walk out?

00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:14.000
First you have a fellow that seems like he's bored with the whole thing.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Then you have the tough guy that enjoys the fight.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Then you have the little guys that go to the fights and they can't stand the sight of blood.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.000
Then you have a big shot gangster, gambler you know, got the fight fixed, third round,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:18.000
the guy's painful as ice.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Then you have the big shot gangster, gambler,

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Then you have the little fella that takes his wife and he never really gets to see the fight,

00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:21.000
but she keeps asking questions.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:47.000
Then you have the big shot gangster, gambler,

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:52.000
The difference between our American institutions and those of our English cousins across the seas

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:58.000
is nowhere more apparent than in the field of radio and television broadcasting.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:15.000
We now present our version of the eminent British newscaster, Lord Beaverhead.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.000
Oh, are you there, hey? I'll be with you presently here.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.000
I've got a bit of news, if I could just find it here.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:30.000
It was here a fortnight ago.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.000
We have a new way of going to condense the news for you, you know,

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.000
going to bring it right to you.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.000
Here's a long article here about a man who gets a divorce from his wife because she talked too much.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:47.000
He's a big one of our meatpackers here, but instead of taking all that time to do that, we will abbreviate it.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Here's the news.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Meatpacker Ken's tongue.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.000
Oh, what happened to my monocle? I think I'll have to use this to find it.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.000
Oh, there it is.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.000
Oh, and I'll bring you the correct time.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:11.000
When you hear the musical note, it will be exactly two o'clock.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:14.000
We put it that way in case we were a bit off, you know.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Listen for the musical note.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.000
Two o'clock, I wonder what time it is.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:29.000
Here's a bit of news that came in.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:33.000
Well, it's coming in again now.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:38.000
Oh, dear me. Looks like we're having a bit of trouble in Egwipe.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:39.000
Egwipe.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.000
E-G-Y-P-E.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:49.000
Oh, Egypt!

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.000
This blasted thing, if I could only keep it in there.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.000
It's clearing up.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:01.000
We have here, you can see, a couple of the Egwipe Egypt battleships.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.000
They were handed to me by the King Farouk.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:14.000
The joke that I had about that has been cut.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:19.000
Oh, well, time for tea. Time for tea.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.000
Little tea never hurt anyone, you know.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.000
There we are. Just slice that around me a bit, you know.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:34.000
Little tea never hurt anyone. There we are.

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.000
Got to sweeten it up a little, you know.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.000
Little tea never hurt anyone, you know.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:45.000
Oh, careful. Don't get nervous yet, boy.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.000
Still no boy.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:53.000
I see here that the Kremlin is going to pave their streets with wooden blocks.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:09:03.000
